            <ArticleTitle>Severe hypoxemia secondary to acute sternal infarction in sickle cell anemia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1617-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article describes a 28-yr-old black man with sickle cell anemia who presented with severe chest pain secondary to acute infarction of the body of the sternum, hypoventilation, and hypoxemia with no evidence of acute chest syndrome. A bone scan performed 5 days after admission revealed increased uptake in the sternum, suggesting sternal infarction. Repeat bone scan performed 2 mo later demonstrated normal concentration in the sternum.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
